MediaGallery 1.8.0 upgrade notes
===============================

MediaGallery 1.8.0 supports upgrades from the 1.7.x line, but it changes where
user media are stored so plugin ZIP replacement can no longer delete them.

Supported baseline
------------------

MediaGallery 1.8.0 requires Geeklog 2.1.1 or newer.

The development branch keeps PHP 5.6-compatible syntax for legacy Geeklog
2.1.1 installations and is also validated against newer PHP versions.

Before upgrading
----------------

1. Back up the Geeklog database.
2. Back up the complete existing MediaGallery media tree.
3. Record any local MediaGallery modifications.
4. Test the upgrade on a staging/local copy first.
5. If upgrading a 1.7.x site whose media still live below
   public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/, run the 1.8 pre-migration tool
   BEFORE uploading the 1.8 plugin ZIP.

Why pre-migration is mandatory for legacy storage
-------------------------------------------------

Geeklog's plugin ZIP updater replaces the old public plugin directory before
MediaGallery's new upgrade code is loaded.

A historical MediaGallery 1.7.x installation commonly stores user data below:

    public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/

Uploading the 1.8 ZIP first could therefore remove the old plugin public tree
before those media have been copied to the new persistent location.

MediaGallery 1.8.0 runtime storage
---------------------------------

Public media are stored below Geeklog's image root:

    $_MG_CONF['path_mediaobjects']
        = rtrim($_CONF['path_images'], '/\\') . '/mediagallery/';

The public URL is derived from Geeklog's image URL configuration.

A normal single-site installation typically resolves to:

    public_html/images/mediagallery/
    https://example.com/images/mediagallery/

A shared-code multisite installation can use site-specific Geeklog image paths,
for example:

    $_CONF['path_images'] = $_CONF['path'] . 'public_html/images/site-a/';
    $_CONF['images_url']  = $_CONF['site_url'] . '/images/site-a';

MediaGallery then uses:

    public_html/images/site-a/mediagallery/
    https://example.com/images/site-a/mediagallery/

Private temporary/upload data are similarly derived from each site's
$_CONF['path_data'].

Pre-migrating a 1.7.x media tree
--------------------------------

Extract the MediaGallery 1.8.0 package on the server without installing it over
the live plugin, then run:

    php tools/migrate-media-storage.php /path/to/geeklog

The tool:

* discovers the legacy MediaGallery media tree;
* copies files into the persistent Geeklog image location;
* verifies copied files by relative path and size;
* refuses conflicting destination files rather than silently overwriting them;
* deliberately leaves the legacy source untouched.

Only after the tool reports a successful migration should the 1.8.0 ZIP be
uploaded through Geeklog's plugin updater.

Fresh 1.8.0 installations
-------------------------

Fresh installations create/use the persistent images/mediagallery storage from
the start. No legacy pre-migration step is required.

Configuration migration
-----------------------

The 1.8.0 upgrade adds missing MediaGallery Configuration API values without
overwriting existing administrator values. Valid zero/false values are
preserved.

The historical manually edited MediaGallery config.php is not restored.
Derived plugin URLs, template locations, media storage paths and similar runtime
values remain derived from Geeklog configuration.

Obsolete fresh-install Flash/FlowPlayer controls are no longer created. Some
harmless legacy rows can remain in upgraded databases until their automatic
removal is proven safe across the full supported upgrade matrix.

Security and behavior changes to test
-------------------------------------

MediaGallery 1.8.0 hardens several mutation/import paths. After an upgrade,
test at least:

* browser image/media upload;
* media edit/delete/move and album cover changes;
* album create/edit/delete;
* FTP and ZIP imports if used;
* Remote Media if used;
* moderation approval/rejection if used;
* batch operations;
* image rotation/watermark/resize if used;
* RSS/podcast feeds;
* comments and search;
* member albums and sidebar blocks.

Batch processing on Geeklog 2.1.1
---------------------------------

Normal actions that start a batch remain protected by Geeklog CSRF validation.
Internal batch continuation/cancellation uses POST plus the server-generated
batch session id and verifies that the batch session belongs to the current
user (with the existing administrator override).

This avoids Geeklog 2.1.1's one-time, referer-bound token expiring during a
multi-step batch while retaining session ownership checks.

Interoperability changes
------------------------

MediaGallery 1.8.0 reports media and album changes through Geeklog lifecycle
APIs. Existing media IDs remain unchanged; albums use namespaced IDs such as:

    album:52

This allows plugins such as IndexNow, XML Sitemap and Hub to react to
MediaGallery changes without querying MediaGallery tables directly.

After upgrade
-------------

Confirm that:

* existing albums/media are visible;
* new uploads are written below the persistent Geeklog image path;
* re-uploading/replacing the plugin ZIP does not remove user media;
* administrator configuration remains intact;
* no unexpected PHP warnings/deprecations appear in the Geeklog error log.

